<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Qashqai confederation</h1><p class="st-meta">1750 CE – 1954 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkic-speaking confederation of nomadic tribes in Fars whose ilkhans were a major political force in southern Iran until exiled in 1954.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Qashqai are Turkic tribes who migrate seasonally between winter pastures near the Gulf and summer pastures in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>. They coalesced as a confederation in the 18th century, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire</a> their chief Jani Agha rose to prominence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Fath Ali Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a> recognised his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-khan-qashqai">Jani Khan</a> as ilkhan in 1818.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The ilkhans ruled through tribal khans and kalantars, controlling migration routes, taxes and armed horsemen. Qashqai women are known for weaving gabbeh rugs and kilims. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-khan-qashqai">Ismail Khan</a> Solat al-Dowleh fought the British-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-persia-rifles">South Persia Rifles</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a> forced the tribes to settle and Solat al-Dowleh died in prison in 1933.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1941 his sons Naser Khan and Khosrow Khan revived Qashqai power and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-mosaddegh">Mohammad Mosaddegh</a>. After the 1953 coup they were exiled in 1954, ending the confederation's political autonomy; the Qashqai people remain a distinct community.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1750 CE – 1954 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56